      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Name: GBIF and both pages use Salvia rosmarinus; the old name Rosmarinus officinalis is a synonym and the ASPCA page still uses it. Look-alikes on NC State: lavender and santolina. Watering: RHS says fairly drought tolerant, container plants need extra water in hot dry spells because the compost dries fast, never waterlogged, no frequency; NC State says low tolerance of wet and humid conditions, overwatering is the common cause of death. let_top_dry is the agent's mapping; interval 7 days is a guess. Pots: RHS recommends at least 30 cm wide and deep, soil-based peat-free compost with up to 25 percent grit, wrap or move container plants in winter. Light: both say full sun (NC State also lists partial shade). Temperature: RHS says established plants survive down to about -8 C; container plants are more vulnerable; NC State gives only USDA zones 8a to 10b. Size: NC State 4 to 5 ft by 3 to 4 ft (about 150 by 120 cm upper values); RHS gives none and notes upright and trailing varieties, so from_knowledge_verify. Growth rate medium (NC State). Humidity: NC State's page contradicts itself, so left unknown. Toxicity: ASPCA non-toxic to cats and dogs; NC State tags agree; neither names agents. Severity 0 is our grading. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
